 "overview": {
  "summary": "The calibrator's ac signal source. A13 generates a sine wave from 0.22 V to 22 V over 10 Hz to 1.1999 MHz, in five frequency ranges (100 Hz, 1 kHz, 10 kHz, 100 kHz, 1 MHz) selected by latching relays K1-K3 and two voltage ranges (2.2 V and 22 V) selected by K4. Everything above 22 V in the instrument starts here: the Power Amplifier, the High Voltage assemblies, the Current function, the Switch Matrix and the Wideband module all take OSC OUT HI from this board. Four blocks make it up. A double-integrator quadrature RC oscillator (U3 summing, U6 and U9 integrating, with resistive DACs U5 and U7 setting frequency inside a range) produces four equal-amplitude phases 90 degrees apart. An amplitude control loop rectifies those four phases through CR1-CR4 and Z6, compares the result with a -12 V reference in error integrator U18, and trims the loop phase through multiplier U16. A phase-locked loop -- zero crossing detectors U23, phase detector U22/U21, charge pumps CR5-CR8, loop filter U31A with range capacitors C75-C84 selected by U17 -- pulls the oscillator onto an external reference from A7 or the rear panel. And a 0.22-22 V gain-controlled output amplifier built round the A13A1 daughter card (U30 on the silkscreen) with a bootstrapped complementary Darlington output stage drives the binding posts. A13 does not set its own amplitude: A12 measures the output and returns OSC CONT, so the two boards are one servo split across a motherboard.",
  "sections": "§2-118, §2-130 … §2-138",
  "note": "A13 makes almost none of its own power. +/-44 S, +/-17 S, +/-15 S, +/-5 LH, LH COM, +5 RLH and RLH COM all arrive from the Regulator/Guard Crossing assembly (A17) on P512. Three rails are local: U2, a 7912, makes -12 S from -17 S; chokes L1 and L2 with C106/C107 make +5 S and -5 S from +5 LH and -5 LH; and R58/R59 divide +15 S down to the +1.4 V bias used by the zero crossing detectors and the loop filter. The digital section runs on +5 LH referenced to LH COM, and the relay drivers on +5 RLH referenced to RLH COM, so those two commons are not SCOM and a meter left on TP1 will read them wrong."
 },
